Character Profiles
Ben (Guy 2: The Planner)
Background: Senior Dev / Engineering Manager candidate at Amazon or Microsoft.
Traits: Hyper-organized, obsessed with metrics, and treats a casual day-hike like a high-stakes sprint planning session. He bought $600 Arc'teryx and Patagonia gear because he wants to "assimilate" and do the Pacific Northwest right.
Core Anxiety: Losing control. His H-1B visa transfer is pending, and his biggest fear about getting lost in the woods isn't a bear attack—it's HR terminating his I-140 process because he no-showed on Monday.
Romantic Strategy: Tries to win Maya over with logic, preparedness, and financial stability.

Leo (Guy 1: The Chill Guy)
Background: UI/UX Designer or SDE 1 at a mid-sized startup.
Traits: Easygoing, chaotic, and completely underprepared. He is hiking in off-brand sneakers and a free graphic tee from a 2019 hackathon. He relies purely on vibes.
Core Anxiety: Burnout. He is "quiet quitting" tech and constantly talks about opening a late-night chai window in Capitol Hill—a dream Ben thinks is financially ruinous.
Romantic Strategy: Effortless charm. He makes Maya laugh and provides a stark, stress-free contrast to Ben’s constant anxiety.

Maya (The Glue)
Background: Data Scientist or Product Manager.
Traits: Dramatically sarcastic, hyper-intelligent, and addicted to true-crime podcasts. She is deeply exhausted by the "Seattle Freeze" and the tech-bro dating scene.
Core Anxiety: Cultural and parental pressure. Her mom is actively texting her arranged marriage biodatas while she is on the trail.
Role: The mediator and the unspoken object of affection. She frequently calls out the absurdity of their situation—namely, that their parents crossed oceans for a better life, and they are voluntarily suffering in a freezing forest.

Clyde (The "Eerie" Local)
Background: A quintessential Pacific Northwest local.
Traits: Unkempt beard, faded flannel, beat-up socks under Birkenstocks, and carries an unlabeled mason jar. He looks exactly like a cryptid or a true-crime antagonist but speaks in ultra-calm, crunchy jargon about native flora.
Role: The red herring. He inadvertently causes all the horror-tension simply by being socially awkward and appearing out of the fog at the worst possible moments.

Thematic Elements & Cutaways
Smash-Cut Flashbacks: The series will use 2-to-3 second visual smash-cuts for comedic contrast. When Maya complains about missing her Bellevue brunch, we see a pristine 2-second slow-motion shot of a mimosa pouring into a glass, before violently cutting back to her swatting a mosquito in the freezing fog.

The Banter: Conversations revolve around the absurdity of their tech jobs versus wilderness survival, critiquing the biodatas Maya's mom sends, and debating Leo's terrible business ideas.

6-Episode Breakdown
Episode 1: The Trailhead (The Setup)
The Plot: The trio arrives at the Mt. Si parking lot. Ben analyzes trail elevation graphs on his Garmin watch while Leo shows up late with a bag of lukewarm samosas. Maya complains about waking up at 6 AM on a Saturday to look at trees.
The Eerie Vibe: As they step onto the trail, Clyde materializes out of the thick mist. Maya instantly profiles him as an apex predator. Clyde mutters something cryptic about "the moisture in the moss" and vanishes into the brush.
The Hook: They nervously laugh it off, hike up, take a group selfie at the summit, and head back down.

Episode 2: The Sun Sets (The Panic)
The Plot: They reach their rented Tesla, exhausted and ready for dinner. Ben tells Leo to unlock the doors. Leo pats his pockets. The key fob is gone.
The Tension: Cell service is dead. The PNW canopy blocks out the remaining sunlight, making it instantly dark and freezing. Ben starts running a root-cause analysis, blaming Leo for systemic negligence. Maya dramatically drafts a final voice note to her parents in India.
The Hook: As they bicker, they hear the distinct, heavy snap of a branch in the woods.

Episode 3: The Witching Hour (The Paranoia)
The Plot: Using phone flashlights, they are forced to hike back up the trail to retrace Leo's steps.
The Tension: Paranoia sets in. Maya recounts local urban legends. Ben tries to stay rational, but his smartwatch loudly notifies him his heart rate is in the "Peak Cardio Zone." They argue over who Maya would theoretically eat first if they starved.
The Hook: Their flashlight beams hit a shadow standing completely still in the middle of the trail ahead. It is Clyde.

Episode 4: The Forager (The Anti-Climax)
The Plot: Face-off in the dark. Ben raises his heavy Yeti water flask like a weapon. Leo steps in front of Maya.
The Reveal: Clyde steps into the light holding a massive, weirdly shaped Chanterelle mushroom. He isn't stalking them; he is an eccentric local foraging for night-blooming fungi.
The Resolution: Clyde offers them some sourdough starter, points out they are walking in circles, and offers to walk them out. The adrenaline drops, leading to a quiet, genuinely vulnerable moment among the three friends.

Episode 5: The Ranger Station (The False Epiphany)
The Plot: Sitting in a heated, brightly lit Ranger Station at 11 PM, drinking terrible coffee.
The Epiphany: Ben opens his $600 jacket to grab his wallet and discovers a massive tear in his inner pocket lining. He suddenly remembers taking the key from Leo earlier to "keep it secure" during a steep incline.
The Meltdown: Ben has a full existential crisis, apologizing profusely to Leo for failing his own risk-management protocols and being a jerk. Leo gives him a laid-back hug, instantly forgiving him. Maya is highly impressed by Ben taking accountability.

Episode 6: The Minivan Ride (The Twist)
The Plot: Clyde drives them back to their car in his rusty 1998 Subaru Outback, which smells entirely of wet earth.
The Dynamic: Maya falls asleep, resting her head on Ben’s shoulder. Ben sits frozen, secretly thrilled, feeling like he finally won the day.
The Twist: Leo is sitting in the very back among buckets of wild mushrooms. He reaches down to adjust his wet sock—and feels hard plastic tucked into the heel of his boot. He pulls out the Tesla key fob. Silas was right; he did drop it in his shoe.
The Ending: Leo looks in the rearview mirror, seeing Maya sleeping peacefully on Ben. Making a split-second decision to let Ben have the win, Leo quietly rolls down the Subaru window and drops the key out into the rainy Pacific Northwest night.
